WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.655
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.655 --> 00:00:05.490
we help a food image sharing
service get performance back

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.020
on track with custom images.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.850
Like your meals in
30 minutes or less?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.460
Then stay tuned.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.370
Last episode, we got a chance
to work with the Foodie Pics

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.530
team who make a social
network that only allows

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.910
you to share pictures of food.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.120
No text, no audio, no emoji,
just a picture of food

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.730
and the Geolotag of
where it was taken at.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.060
Their application uses Compute
Engine instances to process

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.730
and store the uploaded images.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.230
The issue that they've seen is
that their instance boot time

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.230
is through the roof, and
causing latency issues when

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.910
heavy load is on their system.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.910
Now, we've already profiled
the startup time stages,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.220
as well as a bunch of
random configurations,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.650
and we're able to clearly
eliminate the instance boot

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.800
process, chosen image,
and underlying serving

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.024
architecture, as potential
bottlenecks for performance,

00:00:50.024 --> 00:00:51.690
which means it was
time to start digging

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.640
into the code running on the
instance to find the problem.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.640
To do this, we take a
look at the startup script

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.380
that Foodie Pics was using.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.200
Once a new instance was
created, their very first step

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.960
of their app was to execute
a startup script, which

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.660
installed various services
and code libraries that

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.220
would allow the
instance to function

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.810
and be accessible externally.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.070
Their script was
significantly more complex,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.904
but it generally looks
something like this.

00:01:13.904 --> 00:01:15.570
Clearly, though, we
needed to figure out

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.880
what stage of this script was
causing the performance burden.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.260
Now, profiling a startup
script is a weird exercise.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.090
A, how do we get
the timing data,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.660
and B, how do we return that
so that we can test it later?

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.640
For the first problem,
I'm working in Linux

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.020
and dealing with bash scripts.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.960
So the seconds command
works just fine,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.480
wrapping it around each one
of the sections in the script.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.730
For the second problem
of reporting the data,

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.290
there's lots of options here.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.200
Chances are that you've got code
that already takes advantage

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.136
of powerful tool systems
like Statsd or Brubeck,

00:01:46.136 --> 00:01:47.760
but for my purposes,
that would require

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.950
pulling down yet
another package,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.569
not to mention the time required
in starting those services

00:01:52.569 --> 00:01:53.860
before I'd be able to use them.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.380
So I'll skip that for now.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.410
On the other hand, the
Stackdriver custom metrics API

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.190
might work.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.400
See, all GCE instances come
pre-installed with access

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.350
to write to this API,
so that would get rid

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.000
of the need to bring
down new packages

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.950
or host complex back ends.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.780
Sadly, though,
writing to the API

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.420
requires some complexity,
namely authentication,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.010
which I'm not willing to put
forth effort into right now.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.530
So we'll skip that.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.530
For this case, I did something
much more hack-tastic,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.890
store the timings in a file and
serve them as a new endpoint.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.390
This allowed me to
pull the endpoint

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.030
from an external location
and get back my data

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.650
without too much heavy lifting.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.530
Here is a graph showing the
timings for each section

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.500
as 100 iterations of
timing the startup script.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.530
It's clear to see in the
graph that the time per stage

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.630
of the startup script
doesn't fluctuate

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.130
too much between tests.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.070
It's somewhere between
60 and 75 seconds.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.400
No one stage clearly stands
out as a performance problem.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.920
Really, it's the aggregation
of all the stages

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.570
that's creating an issue.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.150
Doing some more digging
with the Foodie Pics' CTO,

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.580
it became clear that they
only updated those scripts

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.640
about once or twice a month at
this point, so the content they

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.640
were pulling down was mostly
static, which pointed almost

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.980
immediately to their problem.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.330
See, each time a new
instance is spun up,

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.050
they're pulling down
almost identical content.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.450
The change is on,
maybe, a monthly basis.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.700
They're basically wasting
a minute of cold boot time

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.710
to do the same thing over,
and over, and over again,

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.760
which means we can
leverage custom images.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.340
A custom image,
or image snapshot,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.370
is basically a snapshot of
the host disk information

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.530
after everything has
already been installed.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.230
When a target VM is booted,
the image information

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.880
is copied right
to the hard drive.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.760
No need to go grab all
that data one at a time

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.714
and install thousands
of files from the net.

00:03:38.714 --> 00:03:40.380
As soon as you boot
up the new instance,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.171
you're just paying for
the performance cost

00:03:42.171 --> 00:03:44.090
of installing the image itself.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.220
For Foodie Pics, this was a
huge performance improvement.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.260
They were able to make
a custom image with most

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.110
of the big packages
installed, but still allowed

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.030
a small startup script
that pulled down

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.530
the server front-end code.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.420
The result was moving
from a boot time

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.860
in minutes down to one
measured in subseconds.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.960
And just a note, even though
we're using a custom image,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.510
the boot phase of installing
their image really

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.760
didn't fluctuate much,
which is fantastic,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.640
considering that we're not only
installing our core operating

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.890
system, but also a
bunch of other software

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.279
which was previously pulled
down via network commands.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:19.829
The Foodie Pics team ran
into a very common problem

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:20.880
for most developers.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.380
Once you reach a certain level
of complexity in your startup

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.740
scripts, the cold boot
time of your application

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.140
starts to suffer, at which
point it's a great idea

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.750
to consider using custom
images to bring down your boot

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.330
time to something normal.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:34.830
So, if you're interested in
more Cloud Performance goodness,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.350
make sure to check out the rest
of our videos and remember,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.040
when it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

